  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.1. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement a new generation of gender-responsive national employment policies, including for youth</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 2 November 2021, the Government of North Macedonia adopted the new National Employment Strategy 2021-2027 (NES, 2027) and the National Action Plan on Employment 2021-2023.

The NES 2027 is aimed at addressing both the short-term labour market consequences of the COVID-19 pandemic, as well as the structural challenges affecting employment growth. The NES and the Action Plan take into consideration both supply and demand side interventions that will be required in that the guiding scenario for the identification of employment policy options for the period 2021-2027 was provided by: (i) the Economic Reform Programme 2021-2023; (ii) the policy priorities established by the Government in the field of education and training, private sector development and social protection; and (iii) available macroeconomic and employment projections. During the first half of 2021, the ILO provided assistance to a tripartite multi-ministerial working group led by the Ministry of Labour and Social Policy. The ILO facilitated a gender-transformative diagnostic, the policy review, and the action planning for the new National Employment Strategy.

The process was accompanied by an extensive capacity development programme for the participating institutions, including the social partners and the economic and social council. 

The work with the ministry and the public employment service on updating the youth guarantee relied significantly on the ILO&#x2019;s facilitation of peer-learning and exchanges with other public employment services through South-South and triangular cooperation (SSTC).</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">3.1.1. Number of member States with new generation of national employment policies addressing country-specific future of work challenges</narrative>
    </title>
